  "notes": [
    "Pins are 3.3 V only. Driving one from a 5 V output will damage the SoC, level shift anything 5 V.",
    "GPIO2 and GPIO3 carry fixed 1.8 kΩ pull-ups to 3V3 on the board, so they cannot be used as plain inputs with an external pull-down.",
    "Each GPIO pin is safe to about 16 mA. The 50 mA figure for the whole header comes from the original Model B; current boards have more headroom on 3V3, but keep GPIO loads small and drive anything bigger through a transistor.",
    "GPIO0/GPIO1 (pins 27/28) are reserved for HAT ID EEPROM detection. Avoid them unless you are building a HAT.",
    "Pin numbering is physical here. BCM (Broadcom) numbers are the pin names; the obsolete WiringPi scheme is a third numbering you will still see in old forum posts."
  ],
